  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "contents": [
        {
          "$type": "text",
          "text": "{\"total_weeks\":9,\"goal_description\":\"Late Summer Half Marathon\",\"phases\":[{\"phase_type\":\"Base\",\"weeks\":3,\"weekly_distance_start_km\":28,\"weekly_distance_end_km\":35,\"intensity_distribution\":\"85/15 easy/hard\",\"allowed_workout_types\":[\"Easy\",\"LongRun\",\"Recovery\",\"CrossTrain\"],\"target_pace_easy_sec_per_km\":378,\"target_pace_fast_sec_per_km\":330,\"notes\":\"Post-injury re-entry. Volume starts below your recent baseline to protect the calf and build connective tissue tolerance. No quality sessions yet. Every run is easy or recovery pace. Long run caps at 14 km by week 3. If the calf gives any feedback, back off that day completely and note what happened. Deload in week 3 drops volume roughly 20% before the Build phase begins.\",\"includes_deload\":true},{\"phase_type\":\"Build\",\"weeks\":4,\"weekly_distance_start_km\":36,\"weekly_distance_end_km\":48,\"intensity_distribution\":\"80/20 easy/hard\",\"allowed_workout_types\":[\"Easy\",\"LongRun\",\"Tempo\",\"Interval\",\"Recovery\",\"CrossTrain\"],\"target_pace_easy_sec_per_km\":378,\"target_pace_fast_sec_per_km\":306,\"notes\":\"Quality work enters on Tuesday and Saturday per your preference. Tuesday carries a tempo or cruise-interval session. Saturday carries a structured workout or a long run with pace work in the final segment. Long run builds toward 18 to 19 km by week 7. All paces use Daniels-Gilbert zones derived from your 10 km time. Easy pace stays at 6:18/km. Tempo targets 5:06/km. Week 7 is a deload week. Volume pulls back before the taper begins.\",\"includes_deload\":true},{\"phase_type\":\"Taper\",\"weeks\":2,\"weekly_distance_start_km\":32,\"weekly_distance_end_km\":22,\"intensity_distribution\":\"85/15 easy/hard\",\"allowed_workout_types\":[\"Easy\",\"LongRun\",\"Tempo\",\"Recovery\"],\"target_pace_easy_sec_per_km\":378,\"target_pace_fast_sec_per_km\":306,\"notes\":\"Volume drops sharply. Intensity is preserved in one short quality session per week to keep the legs sharp without accumulating fatigue. Long run in week 8 is 14 km at easy effort. Race week keeps total volume low. Two or three short easy runs before race day. Do not add volume to compensate for reduced load. The reduction is the work.\",\"includes_deload\":false}],\"rationale\":\"Nine weeks is a tight window for a 1:45 half marathon off a calf strain history, but the math is workable. Your 10 km time of 47:30 puts your pace-zone index at a level that supports a half marathon finish in the 1:43 to 1:47 range, so 1:45 is realistic if you arrive at the start line healthy. The plan prioritizes that. Three weeks of base-only running protects the calf while rebuilding aerobic volume from a conservative starting point. The 10% weekly volume rule is respected throughout. Quality work enters in week 4 when the tissue has had time to adapt. The build phase uses Daniels-Gilbert zones to set all paces, keeping easy runs genuinely easy and hard sessions targeted precisely. The two-week taper follows standard evidence-based practice: volume drops but one quality session per week is retained to prevent staleness. Your IT-band history is noted. Watch hip abductor fatigue during the build phase, particularly as long runs extend past 16 km. IT-band issues at higher volume are often a load management signal, not a structural one.\",\"warnings\":\"This plan assumes no active calf injury at the start date. If the calf is symptomatic when week 1 begins, do not start the plan. Consult a physiotherapist first and return once you have clearance. The plan does not redistribute missed sessions. If a run is missed, leave it and move on. Do not add the distance to another day. Nine weeks leaves little room for forced rest, so conservative early pacing is the main injury-prevention tool. If calf tightness returns at any point during training, treat that session as an easy day or rest day and flag it before continuing structured work.\"}"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
